{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "174",
    "case_summary": "A 37-year-old woman with systemic lupus erythematosus and Sjögren’s syndrome was diagnosed with autoimmune hepatitis and scheduled for upper gastrointestinal endoscopy before starting prednisolone. She had never received acid-secretion inhibitors, including proton pump inhibitors or vonoprazan. Vital signs and physical examination were unremarkable. \n\nEndoscopic findings:\n- Antrum: normal appearance.\n- Gastric corpus: normal coloration with a regular arrangement of collecting venules; red streaks and slight mucosal swelling on the lesser curvature of the angle.\n- Magnified narrow-band imaging of the corpus: regular round pit patterns.\n\nBiopsy sites: greater curvature of the prepyloric area and lesser and greater curvatures of the gastric corpus.\n\nHistologic findings (lesser curvature of corpus):\n- Three defined layers: first (foveolar epithelium) showed foveolar hyperplasia; second (parietal cells) was shortened with mild degeneration; third (chief cells) preserved.\n- Layer contours slightly disorganized with mild lymphocytic infiltration.\n- H+/K+-ATPase staining: heterogeneously reduced.\n- Pepsinogen I: uneven staining.\n- MUC6: positive throughout the region.\n- Chromogranin A: slight enterochromaffin-like cell hyperplasia.\n\nLaboratory tests after endoscopy:\n- Anti-parietal cell antibody: <1:10 (negative)\n- Anti-intrinsic factor antibody: negative\n- Serum gastrin: 150 pg/mL (normal 37–172)\n- Vitamin B12: 870 pg/mL (normal 233–914)\n- Anti-H. pylori IgG: <3 U/mL (negative)\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. autoimmune gastritis\nB. proton pump inhibitor\nC. Parietal cell dysfunction\nD. Helicobacter pylori\nE. None\nF. None\nG. None\nH. None\n",
    "root": {
      "label": "Subacute Gastric Mucosal Inflammation with Autoimmune Features",
